.TH CIRCUITTEST 1
.SH NAME
circuittest \- check circuit(2) against the closed forms
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B circuittest
[
.B -v
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Circuittest
checks
.IR circuit (2)
against circuits whose answers are known in advance, and prints
.B OK
if every one agrees.
.B -v
prints each measured number as well.
.PP
Every circuit here has a closed form, which is the point.
A divider is a ratio.
An RC step is
.I V
(1 \- e\u\s-2\-\fIt\fP/\fIRC\fP\s+2\d) and an RL step the same shape in
current.
A series RLC rings as a damped sinusoid whose frequency and decay rate
are both known, and both are checked, because a scheme can get one and
not the other: backward Euler keeps the frequency about right and damps
far too fast.
An inductor with current already in it and a capacitor with charge
already on it each decay into a resistor as e\u\s-2\-\fIt\fP/\(*t\s+2\d.
Nothing is checked against a previous run of this program.
.PP
The refusals are checked too, and they are half of what makes the rest
mean anything.
A solver that answered for a floating node, or for two voltage sources
argued across each other, would pass every positive check above while
being useless \- so both must be refused, and a circuit that does have
an answer must not be, or the refusals would be satisfied by a solver
that refused everything.
.PP
Two of the checks exist because of a blindness rather than a bug.
With the circuit at rest the inductor's initial-condition stamp is a
current source of zero amps and the capacitor's a source of zero volts,
so a sign error in either is invisible to every other test here.
Only a nonzero initial condition can see it, and the inductive kick \-
1 A through 100 ohms putting \-100 V across a circuit that was at 0 V \-
is large, signed, and could not be produced by an accident.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/circuittest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR circuit (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Each disagreement prints a line beginning
.B FAIL
naming what was measured, what came out and what was wanted; the count
follows and the program raises an exception.
.SH BUGS
Everything checked here is linear with constant sources, because that is
all
.IR circuit (2)
does.
.PP
The accuracy checks run at one step size each.
They would catch a scheme that was wrong, but they do not measure the
order of either method by refining the step and watching the error fall,
so a first-order bug in something billed as second-order would pass if
its error at that one step size were small enough.
The comparison between the two methods is real, though: it is measured
at a step coarse enough that they differ by two orders of magnitude, and
it is checked that backward Euler's error at that step is large enough
for the comparison to mean something.
.PP
Nothing here checks the netlist parser's behaviour on a file rather than
a string, since it takes only a string.
